<?php
return [
    1 => [
        'title' => 'Delirium: Acute Disturbance in Attention and Awareness with Fluctuating Course',
        'slug' => 'delirium-diagnostic-criteria',
        'description' => 'Acute attentional disturbance, fluctuating clouding of consciousness, medical etiologies, and differential distinction from dementia.',
        'overview' => "Delirium represents an acute, severe neurocognitive emergency characterized by a disturbance in attention and awareness that develops rapidly over a short period of time (typically hours to a few days) and represents a marked physiological deviation from baseline baseline functioning. Unlike primary neurodegenerative disorders, delirium is fundamentally fluctuating in its course: patients frequently demonstrate unpredictable variations in alertness and cognitive coherence throughout a 24-hour cycle, often experiencing profound nocturnal exacerbations colloquially referred to as 'sundowning'.\n\nThe core psychopathological feature of delirium is not primarily memory loss, but rather a profound disruption of the attentional apparatus and sensorium. Patients exhibit an inability to direct, focus, sustain, or shift attention, accompanied by reduced orientation to the environment. An individual in a delirious state may drift mid-sentence, fail to follow simple sequential requests, become easily distracted by irrelevant background stimuli, or experience vivid perceptual distortions, including illusions (such as misinterpreting intravenous tubing as serpents) and fleeting visual hallucinations.\n\nFrom an epidemiological and clinical perspective, delirium is invariably a neurobiological manifestation of an underlying medical condition, substance intoxication or withdrawal, toxic medication exposure, or multiple interacting physiological stressors. It is exceptionally prevalent in acute medical-surgical wards, intensive care units (ICU delirium), and post-operative recovery rooms, particularly among geriatric patients with pre-existing mild or major neurocognitive vulnerability. Because delirium carries a significantly increased risk of morbidity, prolonged hospitalization, permanent cognitive decline, and mortality, immediate identification and reversal of the underlying etiology are of paramount clinical urgency.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "DSM-5 Diagnostic Criteria for Delirium:\n\nCriterion A: A disturbance in attention (i.e., reduced ability to direct, focus, sustain, and shift attention) and awareness (reduced orientation to the environment).\n\nCriterion B: The disturbance develops over a short period of time (usually hours to a few days), represents a change from baseline attention and awareness, and tends to fluctuate in severity during the course of a day.\n\nCriterion C: An additional disturbance in cognition (e.g., memory deficit, disorientation, language disturbance, visuospatial ability, or perceptual disturbance).\n\nCriterion D: The disturbances in Criteria A and C are not better explained by another pre-existing, established, or evolving neurocognitive disorder and do not occur in the context of a severely reduced level of arousal, such as coma.\n\nCriterion E: There is evidence from the history, physical examination, or laboratory findings that the disturbance is a direct physiological consequence of another medical condition, substance intoxication or withdrawal (i.e., due to a drug of abuse or to a medication), or exposure to a toxin, or is due to multiple etiologies.\n\nSubtypes by Psychomotor Activity:\n• Hyperactive: Characterized by psychomotor agitation, hypervigilance, restlessness, emotional lability, and disruptive behaviors (frequently observed in alcohol or sedative withdrawal).\n• Hypoactive: Characterized by psychomotor sluggishness, lethargy, apathy, and stupor (frequently misdiagnosed as severe depression or fatigue, especially in elderly post-operative patients).\n• Mixed level of activity: Characterized by alternating periods of hyperactive and hypoactive psychomotor presentations.",
        'specifiers' => "Etiological Specifiers:\n• Substance intoxication delirium: Developed during severe intoxication.\n• Substance withdrawal delirium: E.g., Delirium Tremens in alcohol withdrawal.\n• Medication-induced delirium: E.g., anticholinergic toxicity, polypharmacy.\n• Delirium due to another medical condition: E.g., urinary tract infection (UTI), sepsis, hypoxia, electrolyte disturbance, hepatic encephalopathy.\n• Delirium due to multiple etiologies: Interacting medical and pharmacologic factors.\n\nDuration Specifiers:\n• Acute: Lasting a few hours or days.\n• Persistent: Lasting weeks or months.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Delirium vs. Major Neurocognitive Disorder (Dementia): Delirium exhibits acute onset (hours/days), marked diurnal fluctuation, and prominent disturbance in attention and awareness (clouded sensorium); Major NCD exhibits insidious onset (months/years), stable progressive course, and preserved attention/alertness until advanced terminal stages.\n\nDelirium vs. Depressive Pseudodementia: Hypoactive delirium may resemble severe depression; however, delirium displays acute physiological onset, fluctuating disorientation, and physiological organ/metabolic derangements.\n\nDelirium vs. Psychotic Disorders: In primary schizophrenia, hallucinations are predominantly auditory with clear sensorium and intact attention; in delirium, perceptual disturbances are predominantly visual/tactile and occur alongside profound attentional fragmentation and disorientation.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves an 81-year-old female living independently who was admitted to the hospital following elective hip arthroplasty. On post-operative day two, the nursing staff notes that she suddenly became agitated, pulled out her IV line, claimed the ceiling vents were whispering threats, and could not remember the current year. Three hours later, when examined by the psychiatric consultant, the patient was drowsy, barely responsive to verbal questioning, and unable to count backwards from 20.\n\nPhysical evaluation and laboratory workup revealed urinary retention and an acute Escherichia coli urinary tract infection with elevated inflammatory markers. Following catheterization, intravenous antibiotic therapy, and cautious medication reconciliation, her cognitive sensorium, attention, and orientation completely normalized within 72 hours. This acute, fluctuating presentation meeting Criteria A through E represents Delirium Due to Another Medical Condition (Systemic Infection/UTI), Hypoactive/Hyperactive Mixed Subtype.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: Delirium is defined by acute onset, fluctuating course, and primary disturbance in attention and awareness (not merely memory). It is always secondary to an underlying medical etiology, substance/medication effect, or multiple toxicities. Hypoactive delirium is the most commonly missed presentation in geriatric care. Delirium Tremens occurs 48–96 hours after alcohol cessation. Never diagnose a primary psychotic disorder or new-onset dementia in the presence of acute attentional fluctuation without ruling out medical delirium.",
        'key_concepts' => [
