    10 => [
        'title' => 'Chapter Mastery & Comprehensive Licensure Board Review',
        'slug' => 'ncd-personality-paraphilic-mastery-synthesis',
        'part_type' => 'mastery',
        'description' => 'Comprehensive synthesis of Domain 12: neurocognitive trajectories, Cluster A-C character pathology, and paraphilic forensic benchmarks.',
        'overview' => "This concluding mastery chapter synthesizes the core theoretical architectures, differential diagnostic frameworks, and high-yield licensure examination principles governing Neurocognitive, Personality, and Paraphilic Disorders under DSM-5. Mastery of this domain requires clinicians to demonstrate sophisticated diagnostic discrimination across three distinct clinical axes: acquired organic cognitive decline, enduring developmental character structures, and legal/forensic boundaries in human psychosexuality.\n\nIn the neurocognitive realm, clinicians must command the three-way differential between Delirium, Major Neurocognitive Disorder, and Mild Neurocognitive Disorder. Delirium represents an acute, fluctuating medical disturbance of attention and awareness; Mild NCD represents modest cognitive decline (1 to 2 SD below norms) with preserved IADL independence; and Major NCD represents significant cognitive decline (>= 2 SD below norms) that directly compromises IADL independence. Furthermore, etiology dictates clinical course: Alzheimer's is insidious and memory-encoding predominant; Vascular NCD is stepwise and executive-predominant; and Lewy Body NCD features fluctuating alertness, visual hallucinations, and spontaneous parkinsonism.\n\nIn the personality domain, clinicians must master the general diagnostic criteria (enduring, pervasive, inflexible across >= 2 of 4 functional spheres, with adolescent onset) and the three-cluster system. Crucial board exam contrasts include: Schizoid (no desire for relationships) vs. Avoidant (desires relationships but fears rejection); OCPD (ego-syntonic perfectionism and control) vs. OCD (ego-dystonic intrusive obsessions and compulsions); and ASPD (callous disregard for rights requiring age >= 18 and Conduct Disorder before age 15) vs. BPD (splitting, chronic emptiness, abandonment fear, and suicidal gestures). Finally, in the paraphilic realm, the definitive principle is the two-tier structure: an atypical sexual interest is a Paraphilia, which only becomes a Paraphilic Disorder when Criterion B (subjective distress/impairment OR action involving nonconsenting persons) is satisfied.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Comprehensive Board Review Diagnostic Comparison Matrix:\n\n1. Neurocognitive Continuum:\n• Delirium: Acute onset, fluctuating course, primary impairment in attention and awareness, medical etiology.\n• Mild NCD: Modest decline (1-2 SD), IADL INDEPENDENCE PRESERVED.\n• Major NCD: Significant decline (>= 2 SD), IADL INDEPENDENCE LOST (requires assistance).\n• Alzheimer's Disease: Amyloid plaques, tau tangles, insidious onset, early episodic memory loss.\n• Vascular NCD: Stepwise decline, infarcts on neuroimaging, prominent executive deficits.\n• Lewy Body NCD: Fluctuating alertness, detailed visual hallucinations, spontaneous parkinsonism, neuroleptic sensitivity.\n\n2. Personality Disorders (Enduring, Pervasive, Inflexible, Adolescent Onset):\n• Cluster A (Odd/Eccentric): Paranoid (mistrust), Schizoid (detachment/no desire for bonds), Schizotypal (magical thinking/eccentricity).\n• Cluster B (Dramatic/Erratic): Antisocial (age >= 18, Conduct Disorder < 15, no remorse), Borderline (splitting, emptiness, abandonment fear, self-harm), Histrionic (attention-seeking), Narcissistic (grandiosity, lack of empathy).\n• Cluster C (Anxious/Fearful): Avoidant (longs for bonds, terrified of rejection), Dependent (clinging, cannot make decisions), OCPD (ego-syntonic perfectionism, rules, hoarding, no true OCD obsessions).\n\n3. Paraphilic Disorders (Criterion A >= 6 months + Criterion B Distress or Nonconsenting Harm):\n• Voyeuristic: Age >= 18, viewing unsuspecting naked/undressing individuals.\n• Exhibitionistic: Exposing genitals to unsuspecting strangers.\n• Frotteuristic: Touching/rubbing against nonconsenting persons in public.\n• Pedophilic: Age >= 16 AND at least 5 years older than prepubescent child (age <= 13).\n• Remission Rule: At least 5 years without distress or nonconsenting acts in an uncontrolled environment.",
        'specifiers' => "High-Yield Specifier Review:\n• Delirium: Hyperactive, Hypoactive, Mixed.\n• Major/Mild NCD: Probable vs. Possible; Without vs. With behavioral disturbance.\n• Major NCD Severity: Mild (IADLs impaired), Moderate (basic ADLs impaired), Severe (fully dependent).\n• Pedophilic Disorder: Exclusive vs. Nonexclusive; Attracted to Males, Females, or Both; Limited to incest.\n• Paraphilic Course: In a controlled environment; In full remission (>= 5 years).",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Master Differential Synthesis:\n• Delirium vs. Dementia: Delirium has acute onset, clouding of consciousness/attention, and fluctuating course; Dementia has insidious onset and preserved alertness until late stages.\n• Pseudodementia vs. Alzheimer's: In depressive pseudodementia, onset is acute with mood symptoms, patients answer 'I don't know' on memory tests, and cognition normalizes with antidepressant therapy.\n• Schizoid vs. Avoidant PD: Schizoid individuals do not want relationships; Avoidant individuals desperately want relationships but fear rejection.\n• OCPD vs. OCD: OCPD is ego-syntonic perfectionism without obsessions; OCD is ego-dystonic intrusive obsessions and ritualistic compulsions.\n• ASPD vs. Psychopathy: ASPD is a behavioral DSM-5 disorder; Psychopathy is a forensic personality construct assessed via Hare PCL-R.\n• Paraphilia vs. Paraphilic Disorder: Atypical sexual interest without distress, impairment, or harm to nonconsenting others is a paraphilia, NOT a psychiatric disorder.",
        'clinical_example' => "A master licensure examination scenario involves evaluating three complex diagnostic vignettes:\n\nVignette A: A 74-year-old man with memory loss who manages his own bills and drives safely using a calendar, scoring 1.3 SD below norms in delayed recall. Diagnosis: Mild Neurocognitive Disorder (preserved IADLs).\n\nVignette B: A 38-year-old executive who works 80 hours a week, misses deadlines due to rewriting flawless reports, refuses to delegate tasks unless done his exact way, and views his perfectionism as a virtue without intrusive obsessions. Diagnosis: Obsessive-Compulsive Personality Disorder (OCPD - ego-syntonic).\n\nVignette C: A 26-year-old man who experiences sexual arousal from prepubescent children, has never touched a child, but experiences severe guilt, depression, and social isolation due to his intrusive fantasies. Diagnosis: Pedophilic Disorder (Criterion A met; Criterion B fulfilled through subjective distress).",
        'summary_notes' => "Ultimate Licensure Board Synthesis: Commit to memory the IADL dividing line for Major vs. Mild NCD. Recognize delirium by its acute fluctuating attentional disturbance secondary to a medical etiology. Master the distinction between Schizoid (no desire for relationships) and Avoidant (yearns for intimacy, avoids out of fear). Understand that OCPD is ego-syntonic perfectionism without true obsessions, while OCD is ego-dystonic. Remember that ASPD requires age 18+ and Conduct Disorder before age 15. Finally, remember that Paraphilias require Criterion B (distress, impairment, or action with nonconsenting individuals) to be diagnosed as Paraphilic Disorders, and Pedophilic Disorder requires age 16+ and a 5-year age discrepancy.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'The IADL Dividing Line', 'icon' => '⚖️', 'definition' => 'Major NCD compromises independence in instrumental activities of daily living; Mild NCD preserves independence.'],
            ['title' => 'Cluster A-B-C Distinctions', 'icon' => '🎭', 'definition' => 'Cluster A is Odd/Eccentric, Cluster B is Dramatic/Erratic, and Cluster C is Anxious/Fearful character pathology.'],
